 {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $3f(x)+6g(x)$ are\n\n- $3{a}_{0}+6{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $3{a}_{n}+6{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $3{b}_{n}+6{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of $3f(x)+6g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n3{a}_{0}+6{c}_{0}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c24953c9-584d-5ff1-a2f7-1addba6f623e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417683 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
